Republic of the Philippines DEPARTMENT OF THE INTERIOR AND LOCAL GOVERNMENT A. Francisco Gold Condominium II Bldg, EDSA corner Mapagmahal St., Diliman, Quezon CiW OFFICE OF THE UNDERSECRETARY 02 February 2890 DILG OPINION NO. s. MS. TERESA V. MONTILLA Sangguniang Barangay Member Barangay Laging Handa 17 Scout Rallos 1102 Quezon city Dear Ms. Montilla. This refers to your earlier letter requesting our legal opinion following queries: 2010 on the 1) g) J) 4) Is the Punong Barangay correct in his contention that he is not mandated to follow the resolution issued by the sitio leaders even if said resolution was adopted by the Sangguniang barangay and signed by the punong because according to him, said resolution is merely an expression of opinions/ sentiments; Can the Punong Barangay issue barangay clearances/ permits even when a person/estabffshment is in violation of ordinances/resolu tions; What are the administrative sanctions, if any, that may be imposed for failure to poperly discharge his (Punong Barangay) duties and enf01ee laws and 0MinancesP Which office handles this; and Whether or not, being the first-ranking sangguniang barangay member, you may render assi&tance to your fellow sangguniang barangay members in their committee work. Per your letter, these facts were stated: In January 2002, the Sangguniang Panlungsod of Quezon City passed Ordinance No. SP-1153 entitled: "Prescribing the Sound Proofing of All Business Establishments in the City with Bands, Karaoke Bars, Jazz Bands, Discotheques and Similar Establishments"' Said city ordinance was adopted by your Sangguniang Barangay through Barangay Ordinance No. 005-S-2008, directing, among other things, the Punong Barangay to strictly monitor and enforce the provision of said angguniang Panlungsod Ordinance; ill {

On 10 July 2008, the sitio leaders of Barangay Laging Handa assed Resolution appealing to the Punong Barangay to be cautious and igilant in issuing barangay clearance; The Sangguniang Barangay adopted said Resolution of sitio leaders through Barangay Resolution No. 194, series of 2008; This Barangay Resolution has been disregarded by the Punong a ngay in the issuance of barangay clearances. Hence, your queries. With regard to your first query, please be informed that resolutions are legislative actions of the sanggunian which are temporary in character or merely expressing sentiments and opinions of that legislative body (Garcia vs. GOMELEC, 30 September 1997). A resolution is a statement or verdict, not a w, but is a mere expression of an opinion, or merely the form in which the islative body expresses an opinion and used whenever the legislative body assing it wishes merely to express an opinion concerning some given matter or Thus, Resolution No. 01, series of 2008 of the sitio leaders of Barangay ing Handa, which was adopted through Resolution No. 194, series of 2008 of the Sangguniang Barangay thereat is thus a mere expression of the sitio leaders end the Sangguniang Barangay, respectively, for the Punong Barangay to faithfully implement City Ordinance No. SP-1153. On this point, the Punong parangay is correct in his contention that he js not mandated to follow the lution issued by the sitio leaders as adopted by the Sangguniang Barangay. It is worth to emphasize however that while the subject legislative measure is denominated as a resolution so that the Punong Barangay, as discussed above, is not mandated to follow, Resolution No. 01, series of 2008 "ire€ts the Punong Barangay to strictly monitor and enforce the provision of *ngguniang Panlungsod Ordinance No. SP-1153. Be that as it may, even hout the subject resolution, we wish to point out that Section 389 (b)(l) of the ocal Government Code of 1991 (RA 7160) provides that the Punong Barangay mandated to "(E)nforce all laws and ordinances which are applicable within the Panga/' and his failure to abide by this provision of the Code is tantamount to a meglect of duty which is a ground for the filing of administrative charges against im pursuant to Section 60 of the Code. Anent your second query, may we invite your attention to Section 152 (c) Local Govemment Code of 1991 , which provides that: "SEC. 152. Scope of Taxing Powers.— The Barangays may levy taxes, fees, and charges, as provided in this Article, which shall exclusively accrue to them: (c) BaranßY Clearance. — No city or municipality may issue any license or permit for any business or activity unless a cieamnce is dmt obtained from the bamngay where such business or activity is located or conducted. For such cieamnce, the sanguuiang baranßY may impose a reasonable fee. The appHcation for clearance shall be acted upon within seven (7) worHng daye from the filing thereof. In the event that the clearance is not issued within the said period, the city or municipality may issue the said license or permit."

It is discernible from the above-mentioned provision of the Code that an pplication for barangay clearance may be denied by the barangay. However, pode did not mention the instances wherein an application for barangay nce may be refused by the punong barangay. Io this regard, it is our view that if there is an ordinance violated, the Punong Barangay cannot issue a Barangay Clearance. With regard to resolutions, not being a law, violation thereof cannot be used as basis in denying toe Barangay Clearance unless the resolution be made an ordinance. {With regard to your third query, please be informed that Section 60 of the Governm nt Code of 1991 enumerates the grounds for disciplinary actions against elective local officials. The failure of the Punong Barangay to discharge his puties and responsibilities may give rise to an administrative charge for "Pp liction of Duty". In your case, the Sangguniang Panlungsod of Quezon City •and the Ofice of the Ombudsman have concurrent jurisdiction over ad nistrative complaints filed against any elective barangay official thereat, ever first cquired jurisdiction. önent e last issue, we see no legal impediment in the rendition of i$ance by the first-ranking sangguniang barangay member to his/her leagues with respect to the latteds committee work as long as the same is for ppveme t of the barangay or for the welfare of the inhabitants therein. We ho tlat we have addressed your concem accordingly. Very truly yours, AUSTERE A. PANADE Undersecreta , iUSEC EDUARDO R. SOLMAN, JR. DILG-Natio I CapitA Region CLGOO Qwzon City i,il..i
